":5,"length":839},{"pos":329271,"endPos":330428,"startLine":7327,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":7353,"endLineOffset":5,"length":1158},{"pos":330434,"endPos":331630,"startLine":7355,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":7382,"endLineOffset":5,"length":1197},{"pos":331636,"endPos":333295,"startLine":7384,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":7417,"endLineOffset":5,"length":1660},{"pos":333301,"endPos":335312,"startLine":7419,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":7456,"endLineOffset":5,"length":2012},{"pos":335318,"endPos":335967,"startLine":7458,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":7473,"endLineOffset":5,"length":650}]}]}],"groundTruthGeneratedCommentsNodes":[{"groundTruth":{"original_line":1844,"line":1844,"comment_lines":["[1844][]"],"review_comment":["Notice that here you are losing a performance aspect: `mv_expand` is applied on all documents. The idea with a sort (and the **default** pushed down `limit`) in front of `mv_expand` was to have fewer documents to expand from because the expansion is, theoretically, creating even more rows. Before this PR, each node was getting 1000 docs, mv_expanded them and a final sort performed. With this PR, each node is expanding everything, then sorting.","Yes, but you were also discarding all the employees after the first 1000 (sorted by emp_no), that is not what the query says, so technically you got a faster execution, but a wrong result."],"path":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java","id":36731,"created_at":"2025-01-30T13:48:44Z","original_position":6,"side":"LEFT","position":14,"submitted_on_commit":"7ec35340c310ebde975531d609e35f61c4807bdf","diff_hunk":"@@ -1839,10 +1839,9 @@ public void testCombineOrderByThroughFilter() {\n \n     /**\n      * Expected\n-     * TopN[[Order[first_name{f}#170,ASC,LAST]],1000[INTEGER]]\n-     *  \\_MvExpand[first_name{f}#170]\n-     *    \\_TopN[[Order[emp_no{f}#169,ASC,LAST]],1000[INTEGER]]","submitted_line":1844},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"The test cases have been updated to reflect the new rule `RemoveRedundantSort`. However, some of these tests seem to be checking for the presence of a `TopN` node after optimization. Given that the new rule is designed to remove redundant sorts, it might be more appropriate to verify that certain `OrderBy` nodes are no longer present in the optimized plan rather than just checking for the presence of `TopN`. This would provide stronger assurance that the rule is functioning as intended.","nodes":[{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java-101038:101083,101085:101140,101149:101185-100208:100247","promptId":"#YH5ET","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java","dstPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java","srcChanges":[{"pos":101038,"endPos":101083,"startLine":2370,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":2370,"endLineOffset":53,"length":46},{"pos":101085,"endPos":101140,"startLine":2370,"startLineOffset":55,"endLine":2370,"endLineOffset":110,"length":56},{"pos":101149,"endPos":101185,"startLine":2371,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":2371,"endLineOffset":44,"length":37}],"dstChanges":[{"pos":100208,"endPos":100247,"startLine":2355,"startLineOffset":8,"endLine":2355,"endLineOffset":47,"length":40}]},{"id":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java-x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimizer/LogicalPlanOptimizerTests.java-87712:87720,87728:87790,87799:87884,87895:87924,87937:87991,88006:88078,88701:88737,88747:88814,88823:88872,88881:88916-87567:87626,87634:87717,87727:87777,87789:87862,88485:88520","promptId":"#SGQ6W","srcPath":"x-pack/plugin/esql/src/test/java/org/elasticsearch/xpack/esql/optimi